the pointer is used in printf(%s) BEFORE free, but the swap-remove leaves no dangling pointer in the array. However, the freed chunk enters tcache/fastbin and can be reallocated by create_cookie.\"},{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(%llu)\",\"operation\":\"READ/WRITE msg[cookie_index] with size msg_size[cookie_index]-1\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"User-controlled index reaches heap pointer dereference. Index check is only upper-bound (index < cookie_num), no lower-bound check. Since index is unsigned (%llu), negative values wrap to large unsigned values but would fail the upper-bound check. However, if a freed chunk's pointer remains accessible (UAF scenario from eat_cookie swap-remove edge cases), edit could write to freed memory.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(%llu)\",\"operation\":\"READ msg[cookie_index] via printf(%s)\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"User-controlled index reaches heap pointer dereference for output. printf(%s) dereferences msg[index] and prints until NUL. If msg[index] points to freed/reallocated memory, this could leak heap or libc addresses.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"message_length via scanf(%lld)\",\"operation\":\"ALLOC calloc(message_length,1) and READ into it\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"User-controlled allocation size (up to 256) and user-controlled read content. Size is bounded (<=256). calloc zeroes memory. This is the allocation path that can reuse freed chunks from eat_cookie.\"},{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_index\",\"operation\":\"printf(%s) on msg[random_index] before free\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"The content of the cookie is printed before free. If the cookie content was previously overwritten with heap metadata (via UAF edit), this could leak heap addresses.\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is PIE enabled, 64-bit x86, dynamically linked, not stripped\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Full RELRO, NX enabled, Stack Canary, PIE enabled. No FORTIFY.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is glibc 2.39 (Ubuntu GLIBC 2.39-0ubuntu8.8) at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg array at 0x4060, 32 elements x 8 bytes = 256 bytes, stores char* heap pointers, in .bss\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size array at 0x4160, 32 elements x 4 bytes = 128 bytes, stores int sizes paired with msg[], in .bss\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"cookie_num at 0x41e8, 8 bytes, stores current cookie count, initialized to 5 by init()\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"urandom at 0x41e0, 8 bytes, stores FILE* from fopen(/dev/urandom, 'r')\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() creates 5 initial cookies via strdup with hardcoded strings and sizes: msg[0]=strdup(fortune1) size=0x50, msg[1]=strdup(fortune2) size=0x30, msg[2]=strdup(fortune3) size=0x18, msg[3]=strdup(fortune4) size=0x38, msg[4]=strdup(fortune5) size=0x40\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num <= 0x1f (31) and message_length <= 0x100 (256), uses calloc(length,1) then read(0,buf,length)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie reads 1 byte from /dev/urandom, computes random_index = byte % cookie_num, prints msg[random_index] via printf(%s), frees msg[random_index], then swaps msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index] and msg_size[cookie_num-1] into msg_size[random_index], decrements cookie_num\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, free(msg[random_index]) is called but the pointer is NOT set to NULL before the swap-overwrite. The swap copies msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index], overwriting the freed pointer value\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu), checks only upper bound: index < cookie_num. No lower-bound (negative) check. Since %llu is unsigned, negative input wraps to large values that fail upper-bound check.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads msg_size[index], subtracts 1, uses result as read() size: read(0, msg[index], msg_size[index]-1). The read size is derived from the msg_size array entry paired with the msg pointer.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads index via scanf(%llu), checks only upper bound: index < cookie_num. No lower-bound check. Prints msg[index] via printf(%s).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"main loop: init() once, then loop { menu(), scanf(%lld, &choice), dispatch choice 1-5 }. Choice 5 calls exit(0).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) is set in init(), providing a 60-second timeout\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is at 0x3f58, size 0xa8, writable=false after RELRO (Full RELRO means GOT is read-only at runtime)\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg array (0x4060, 256 bytes) and msg_size array (0x4160, 128 bytes) are adjacent in .bss. msg ends at 0x4160, msg_size starts at 0x4160. msg_size ends at 0x41e0. urandom is at 0x41e0. cookie_num is at 0x41e8.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F019\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"Layout in .bss: stdout(0x4020) -> stdin(0x4030) -> stderr(0x4040) -> completed.0(0x4048) -> msg[0..31](0x4060-0x415f) -> msg_size[0..31](0x4160-0x41df) -> urandom(0x41e0) -> cookie_num(0x41e8). Total .bss size 0x1d0 (464 bytes).\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F020\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie uses swap-remove deletion: the last element (msg[cookie_num-1]) is moved to the freed slot (msg[random_index]). This means if random_index == cookie_num-1 (last element selected), the swap is a self-assignment and the freed pointer remains in the slot (dangling pointer).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F021\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"description\":\"When random_index == cookie_num-1 in eat_cookie: free(msg[cookie_num-1]) is called, then msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[cookie_num-1] (self-assignment, no-op), then cookie_num--. The freed pointer remains in msg[old_cookie_num-1] which is now outside the valid range [0, cookie_num-1]. However, edit_cookie and read_cookie check index < cookie_num, so this slot cannot be directly accessed after decrement.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F022\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie use scanf(%llu) for index input, which reads unsigned long long. The index comparison is: cmp rdx, rax; jl _abort (signed less-than). Since rdx (user input) is compared with rax (cookie_num) using signed jl, if user inputs a value with bit 63 set (very large unsigned), it would be interpreted as negative in signed comparison and pass the check.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether the swap-remove in eat_cookie can lead to a usable UAF (use-after-free) scenario\",\"reason\":\"When random_index != cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is overwritten by the swap. When random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer remains but is outside the valid index range. Need S2 to analyze if any code path can access the freed slot after decrement.\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether the signed comparison jl in edit_cookie/read_cookie allows negative index access\",\"reason\":\"scanf(%llu) reads unsigned, but the comparison uses signed jl. If a very large unsigned value (bit 63 set) is entered, it would be negative in signed comparison and pass index < cookie_num. This could allow OOB access to memory before the msg array. Need S2 to confirm exploitability.\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether tcache poisoning or fastbin attack is feasible with glibc 2.39\",\"reason\":\"glibc 2.39 has tcache with safe-linking (pointer mangling). The freed chunks from eat_cookie enter tcache. create_cookie uses calloc which skips tcache for zeroed allocations. Need S2 to analyze if calloc can reuse tcache chunks or if other allocation paths exist.\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"Whether the msg_size[index]-1 calculation in edit_cookie can cause issues when msg_size[index] is 0\",\"reason\":\"If msg_size[index] is 0, then 0-1 = -1 (0xffffffff as unsigned), which would cause read() to attempt reading a very large amount. Need S2 to check if msg_size can be 0.\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"CET (IBT/SHSTK) status unknown\",\"reason\":\"checksec did not report CET flags. Need to check if the CPU/kernel supports CET and if the binary uses it.\"},{\"id\":\"U006\",\"description\":\"Whether heap addresses or libc addresses can be leaked via read_cookie or eat_cookie printf\",\"reason\":\"If a freed chunk's pointer is accessible and the freed chunk contains heap metadata (fd/bk pointers), printf(%s) would print until NUL, potentially leaking addresses. Need S2 to analyze the heap state after free.\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 LSB PIE, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped, GCC 10.2.0, source: fortunecookie.c. Entry: 0x1110. 11 program headers, 29 sections.\",\"sections\":\".text(0x1110,0x891,RX) .rodata(0x2000,0x266,R) .data(0x4000,0x10,RW) .bss(0x4020,0x1d0,RW) .got(0x3f58,0xa8,RW) .plt(0x1020,0xe0,RX) .init(0x1000,0x17,RX) .fini(0x19a4,0x9,RX) .init_array(0x3d58,0x8,RW) .fini_array(0x3d60,0x8,RW) .eh_frame(0x22e0,0x1e0,R) .eh_frame_hdr(0x2268,0x74,R)\",\"symbols\":\"Key symbols: main(0x187f) init(0x1232) create_cookie(0x1399) eat_cookie(0x14e1) edit_cookie(0x1628) read_cookie(0x1751) menu(0x17fd) _abort(0x11f5). Globals: msg(0x4060,256B) msg_size(0x4160,128B) cookie_num(0x41e8,8B) urandom(0x41e0,8B) stdout(0x4020) stdin(0x4030) stderr(0x4040).\",\"disassembly\":\"init: setvbuf x3, alarm(60), fopen(/dev/urandom)->urandom, strdup x5 -> msg[0..4], msg_size[0..4] set, cookie_num=5. create_cookie: check cookie_num<=31, scanf(%lld,size), check size<=256, calloc(size,1), read(0,buf,size), msg[cookie_num]=buf, msg_size[cookie_num]=size, cookie_num++. eat_cookie: check cookie_num!=0, fread(urandom,1,1)->idx, idx%=cookie_num, printf(%s,msg[idx]), free(msg[idx]), msg[idx]=msg[cookie_num-1], msg_size[idx]=msg_size[cookie_num-1], cookie_num--. edit_cookie: printf(Which cookie[0-%d],cookie_num-1), scanf(%llu,idx), check idx<cookie_num(signed jl), read(0,msg[idx],msg_size[idx]-1). read_cookie: printf(Which cookie[0-%d],cookie_num-1), scanf(%llu,idx), check idx<cookie_num(signed jl), printf(%s,msg[idx]). main: init(), loop{menu(),scanf(%lld,choice),dispatch 1-5}.\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_INDEX_CHECK\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie read the cookie index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") as unsigned long long, but validate it against cookie_num using a signed comparison (jl / 'jump if less', signed). When the user supplies a value with bit 63 set (e.g. 0x8000000000000000), the value is interpreted as a large negative number in the signed comparison, so 'idx < cookie_num' evaluates to true and the bounds check is bypassed. The index is then used to access the msg[] and msg_size[] global arrays without any lower-bound validation, resulting in out-of-bounds array access.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[0..31] at 0x4060 (char* array, 256 bytes)\",\"msg_size[0..31] at 0x4160 (int array, 128 bytes)\",\"stdout at 0x4020 (FILE*)\",\"stdin at 0x4030 (FILE*)\",\"stderr at 0x4040 (FILE*)\",\"urandom at 0x41e0 (FILE*)\",\"cookie_num at 0x41e8 (long)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") in edit_cookie and read_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"main\",\"data_flow\":[\"User input via stdin -> scanf(\\\"%llu\\\", &idx) stores unsigned long long at stack [rbp-0x10]\",\"idx loaded into rdx at 0x1676 (edit_cookie) / 0x179f (read_cookie)\",\"cookie_num loaded into rax at 0x167a (edit_cookie) / 0x17a3 (read_cookie)\",\"cmp rdx, rax; jl _abort — signed comparison at 0x1681/0x1684 (edit_cookie) / 0x17aa/0x17ad (read_cookie)\",\"If bit 63 of idx is set: signed interpretation is negative, jl passes, bounds check bypassed\",\"In edit_cookie: msg_size[idx] read at 0x16a7-0x16b6 (lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,msg_size; mov eax,[rdx+rax]) — OOB read from msg_size array\",\"In edit_cookie: msg_size[idx]-1 sign-extended via movsxd at 0x16bc, used as read() size at 0x16de\",\"In edit_cookie: msg[idx] read at 0x16c3-0x16d2 (lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,msg; mov rax,[rcx+rax]) — OOB read of pointer from msg array\",\"In edit_cookie: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) at 0x16de — OOB write to whatever address was read from msg[idx]\",\"In read_cookie: msg[idx] read at 0x17bb-0x17ce — OOB read of pointer\",\"In read_cookie: printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) at 0x17e1 — OOB dereference and data output\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as 0 <= idx < cookie_num using unsigned comparison (jb / jae) to prevent both negative and excessive indices\",\"actual\":\"Index is compared with cookie_num using signed jl (jump if less, signed). Only the upper bound is checked, and the comparison is signed rather than unsigned.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (idx >= 0) AND incorrect comparison type: signed jl instead of unsigned jb. The scanf format %llu reads unsigned, but the comparison treats the value as signed, creating a signedness mismatch that allows negative-index OOB access.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array read and write via negative index\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684 (edit_cookie bounds check), 0x16a7-0x16de (edit_cookie OOB access), 0x17aa-0x17ad (read_cookie bounds check), 0x17bb-0x17e1 (read_cookie OOB access)\",\"expression\":\"msg[idx] and msg_size[idx] where idx is attacker-controlled with bit 63 set\",\"target\":\"msg[] array at 0x4060 (8-byte elements) and msg_size[] array at 0x4160 (4-byte elements), with negative idx accessing memory before these arrays in .bss\",\"size\":\"Depends on idx value; msg[idx] reads 8 bytes, msg_size[idx] reads 4 bytes, read() writes up to msg_size[idx]-1 bytes\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, a negative index causes msg[idx] to read an 8-byte value from memory before the msg array (e.g., FILE* pointers at 0x4020-0x4048, or urandom/cookie_num at 0x41e0-0x41e8 if accessing msg_size with appropriate offset). This value is then passed to printf(\\\"%s\\\") which dereferences it and prints until NUL, potentially leaking memory contents or crashing.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, a negative index causes msg[idx] to read an 8-byte value from memory before the msg array, and msg_size[idx] to read a 4-byte value. The read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) call then writes attacker-controlled data to whatever address was read from msg[idx]. If msg[idx] resolves to a FILE* pointer value, this writes to the FILE structure or other sensitive global.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) with a negative idx dereferences a pointer read from OOB memory. If that pointer happens to point to heap metadata or libc data, the output could leak heap or libc addresses.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If msg[idx] with a negative index reads a value that is not a valid pointer, the subsequent dereference (printf %s or read()) will cause SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1665-0x1671\",\"description\":\"scanf called with format string \\\"%llu\\\" (at .rodata 0x21c4), reading unsigned long long into stack [rbp-0x10]\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1676-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Index loaded into rdx, cookie_num loaded into rax, compared with 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 1692'. jl is a signed comparison (jump if less). A value with bit 63 set is negative in signed interpretation, so it passes the 'idx < cookie_num' check.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] accessed via 'lea rdx,[rax*4+0]; lea rax,[rip+msg_size]; mov eax,[rdx+rax*1]'. With negative idx, this reads 4 bytes from before msg_size array (0x4160), accessing the tail of msg[] array or beyond.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3-0x16de\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] accessed via 'lea rcx,[rax*8+0]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rcx+rax*1]' then passed to read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1). With negative idx, this reads an 8-byte pointer from before msg array (0x4060), potentially hitting stderr(0x4040), stdin(0x4030), stdout(0x4020) FILE* pointers.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x178e-0x179a\",\"description\":\"scanf called with format string \\\"%llu\\\" (at .rodata 0x21c4), reading unsigned long long into stack [rbp-0x10]\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x179f-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Index loaded into rdx, cookie_num loaded into rax, compared with 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 17bb'. Same signed comparison vulnerability as edit_cookie.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] accessed via 'lea rdx,[rax*8+0]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rdx+rax*1]' then passed to printf(\\\"%s\\\"). With negative idx, OOB pointer is dereferenced for output.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060/0x4160\",\"description\":\"msg array at 0x4060 (32 x 8 bytes = 256 bytes, ends at 0x4160) and msg_size at 0x4160 (32 x 4 bytes = 128 bytes, ends at 0x41e0). Memory before msg (0x4060) contains: completed.0 (0x4048), stderr (0x4040), stdin (0x4030), stdout (0x4020). A negative index of -1 accesses msg[0x4058], -2 accesses msg[0x4050], -3 accesses stderr (0x4040), -4 accesses stdin (0x4030), -5 accesses stdout (0x4020). These are FILE* pointers whose values point into libc.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") with input like 9223372036854775808 (0x8000000000000000) actually passes the signed jl check at runtime\",\"Verify what memory contents are at negative offsets from msg[] and msg_size[] arrays in .bss\",\"Determine if the OOB-read pointer values (FILE* pointers) lead to useful memory access or immediate crash\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"INTEGER_UNDERFLOW_IN_EDIT_COOKIE_SIZE_CALCULATION\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Integer Underflow\",\"description\":\"In edit_cookie, the read size for editing a cookie is computed as msg_size[index] - 1. The msg_size[index] value is a 32-bit int read from the msg_size[] array. If msg_size[index] is 0 (which is possible when create_cookie is called with message_length=0, since scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") reads 0 and the check 'cmp rax,0x100; jle' allows 0), then 0 - 1 = -1 (0xFFFFFFFF as unsigned int). This value is then sign-extended via 'movsxd rdx, eax' to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, producing an extremely large read() size parameter.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"create_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size[0..31] at 0x4160 (int array)\",\"heap buffer pointed to by msg[index]\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"message_length=0 via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") in create_cookie, then edit_cookie on that index\",\"entry_function\":\"main\",\"data_flow\":[\"User calls create_cookie (menu choice 2)\",\"scanf(\\\"%lld\\\", &message_length) reads 0 into stack [rbp-0x18]\",\"cmp rax, 0x100; jle passes (0 <= 256) at 0x13f6-0x13fc\",\"calloc(0, 1) called at 0x1416 — returns a valid minimal heap pointer\",\"read(0, buf, 0) reads 0 bytes at 0x1443\",\"msg[cookie_num] = buf (heap pointer) at 0x1489\",\"msg_size[cookie_num] = 0 (truncated from 64-bit 0 to 32-bit int 0) at 0x14a9\",\"cookie_num++ at 0x14c3\",\"Later, user calls edit_cookie (menu choice 3) with the index of the size-0 cookie\",\"msg_size[index] read as int 0 at 0x16b6\",\"sub eax, 0x1 -> eax = 0xFFFFFFFF (-1 as int) at 0x16b9\",\"movsxd rdx, eax -> rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF at 0x16bc\",\"read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) at 0x16de — attempts to read ~2^63 bytes into heap buffer\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"The edit read size should be validated to be > 0 before use, or msg_size should be guaranteed to be >= 1\",\"actual\":\"No validation of msg_size[index] before subtraction. The subtraction msg_size[index] - 1 is performed unconditionally, and the result is sign-extended to 64 bits without any bounds check.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing check for msg_size[index] == 0 (or <= 0) before computing msg_size[index] - 1. No validation that the computed read size is reasonable.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Heap buffer overflow via oversized read()\",\"address\":\"0x16b6-0x16de\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[index], (size_t)(int)(msg_size[index] - 1)) where msg_size[index] == 0\",\"target\":\"Heap buffer pointed to by msg[index], allocated via calloc(0, 1) — a minimal-size heap chunk\",\"size\":\"0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (effectively unlimited) bytes attempted to read into a minimal heap buffer\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"When msg_size[index] is 0, the read() call attempts to write up to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF bytes into a heap buffer allocated with calloc(0,1) (minimal size). The read() system call will write until the input stream ends or an error occurs, massively overflowing the heap buffer and corrupting adjacent heap metadata and objects.\"},{\"type\":\"DATA_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The heap overflow corrupts adjacent heap chunks, heap metadata, and potentially other allocated cookie message buffers.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The overflow will eventually hit unmapped memory or corrupt heap metadata sufficiently to cause a crash in subsequent heap operations.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f2-0x13fc\",\"description\":\"message_length from scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") compared with 'cmp rax,0x100; jle 140a'. jle is signed less-or-equal, so 0 passes the check. Size 0 is allowed.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x140a-0x1416\",\"description\":\"calloc(message_length, 1) called with message_length=0. calloc(0,1) returns a valid pointer to a minimal heap chunk (implementation-defined, typically 0 or minimum allocation size).\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1498-0x14a9\",\"description\":\"msg_size[cookie_num] = (int)message_length. When message_length is 0, msg_size entry is set to 0. The 64-bit value 0 is stored as a 32-bit int via 'mov ecx,edx; mov DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1],ecx'.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16bc\",\"description\":\"msg_size[index] read as 32-bit int (DWORD) at 0x16b6, then 'sub eax,0x1' at 0x16b9, then 'movsxd rdx,eax' at 0x16bc. When msg_size[index]==0: eax=0, sub->eax=0xFFFFFFFF, movsxd->rdx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d6-0x16de\",\"description\":\"The sign-extended value 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is used as the count parameter to read(0, msg[index], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) at 0x16de. This is a heap buffer overflow: writing up to 2^63-1 bytes into a minimal heap allocation.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that calloc(0,1) returns a non-NULL pointer in glibc 2.39\",\"Verify that read() with a huge count actually writes data beyond the heap buffer bounds\",\"Determine how much data read() will actually accept before blocking or erroring\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"HEAP_USE_AFTER_FREE_VIA_EAT_COOKIE_SWAP_REMOVE_SELF_ASSIGNMENT\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, when the randomly selected index equals cookie_num-1 (the last element), the swap-remove operation performs a self-assignment: free(msg[cookie_num-1]) is called first, then msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[cookie_num-1] (a no-op self-copy), then cookie_num is decremented. The freed pointer remains stored in msg[old_cookie_num-1]. After decrement, this slot is at index cookie_num (the new, decremented value), which is outside the valid range [0, cookie_num-1]. While edit_cookie and read_cookie check index < cookie_num (preventing direct access under normal conditions), the signedness mismatch in BUG-001 can be used to access this out-of-range slot with a negative index, or create_cookie can re-increment cookie_num and overwrite the slot before it is accessed. The UAF is only exploitable in combination with BUG-001 or through careful sequencing of eat/create operations.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg[random_index] at 0x4060+offset (dangling pointer after free)\",\"freed heap chunk in tcache/fastbin\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_index from /dev/urandom (not directly attacker-controlled, but outcome is probabilistic)\",\"entry_function\":\"main\",\"data_flow\":[\"eat_cookie reads 1 byte from /dev/urandom, computes random_index = byte % cookie_num\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[random_index]) prints cookie content (before free, safe)\",\"free(msg[random_index]) frees the heap chunk at 0x158e\",\"msg[cookie_num-1] is read and written to msg[random_index] at 0x15b0-0x15c6\",\"When random_index == cookie_num-1: msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[cookie_num-1] (self-assignment, freed pointer remains)\",\"msg_size[cookie_num-1] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] (self-assignment at 0x15e7-0x15fc)\",\"cookie_num-- at 0x1606-0x160a\",\"The freed pointer remains in msg[old_cookie_num-1], now at index == new cookie_num (out of valid range)\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After freeing a pointer, it should be set to NULL or the slot should be properly cleared to prevent any dangling pointer from remaining accessible\",\"actual\":\"No NULLing of the freed pointer. The swap-remove operation is intended to overwrite the freed slot, but when random_index == cookie_num-1, the swap is a self-assignment and the freed pointer survives.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing check for random_index == cookie_num-1 (self-swap case). Missing NULL assignment after free.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Free followed by dangling pointer retention via self-assignment\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free call), 0x15b0-0x15c6 (swap msg), 0x15e7-0x15fc (swap msg_size), 0x1606-0x160a (cookie_num--)\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[idx]); msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; // when idx == cookie_num-1, this is msg[idx] = msg[idx] (no-op after free)\",\"target\":\"msg[cookie_num-1] slot retains freed heap pointer\",\"size\":\"8 bytes (one pointer slot)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"If the dangling pointer slot can be accessed (via BUG-001 signedness bypass or by re-incrementing cookie_num through create_cookie without overwriting the slot), reading msg[old_idx] would dereference a freed heap chunk, potentially leaking heap metadata (fd/bk pointers in tcache).\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"If the dangling pointer slot can be accessed via edit_cookie (through BUG-001 or slot reuse), writing to msg[old_idx] would write to freed heap memory, corrupting heap metadata.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"THEORETICAL\",\"reason\":\"If read_cookie can access the dangling pointer (via BUG-001), printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[old_idx]) would print freed chunk contents, potentially leaking heap addresses stored in tcache fd pointers.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"free(msg[random_index]) called at 0x158e. The pointer is not set to NULL before or after the free.\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1593-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"Swap operation: msg[cookie_num-1] is read (0x15b0) and written to msg[random_index] (0x15c6). When random_index == cookie_num-1, this is a self-assignment: the freed pointer is read from and written back to the same slot, leaving the dangling pointer in place.\"},{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15e7-0x15fc\",\"description\":\"Same self-assignment pattern for msg_size: msg_size[cookie_num-1] written to msg_size[random_index]. When random_index == cookie_num-1, this is also a no-op.\"},{\"id\":\"E017\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1606-0x160a\",\"description\":\"cookie_num decremented after swap. The freed pointer at msg[old_cookie_num-1] is now at index == new_cookie_num, outside the valid range [0, new_cookie_num-1].\"},{\"id\":\"E018\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x14e1\",\"description\":\"The probability of random_index == cookie_num-1 is 1/cookie_num per eat_cookie call. With cookie_num=5 initially, probability is 1/5 = 20% per call. The random byte from /dev/urandom is not attacker-controlled, but the condition is probabilistically achievable.\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that when random_index == cookie_num-1, the self-assignment truly leaves the freed pointer accessible\",\"Determine if BUG-001's signedness bypass can reach the out-of-range slot containing the dangling pointer\",\"Verify whether create_cookie's re-increment of cookie_num overwrites the dangling slot before it can be accessed\",\"Check if glibc 2.39 tcache safe-linking prevents useful exploitation of the freed chunk's metadata\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"User-controlled allocation size and message content\",\"reason\":\"Size is bounded to <= 256 via signed comparison 'cmp rax,0x100; jle' (0x13f6-0x13fc). calloc zeroes the buffer. read() uses the same bounded size. The size 0 case is handled in BUG-002. For sizes 1-256, the allocation and read are correctly matched. No overflow in the allocation path itself.\"},{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"Menu choice dispatch via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\")\",\"reason\":\"Menu choice is compared with exact values (cmp rax,1/2/3/4/5) using jne. No array indexing or memory access based on menu choice. Invalid choices simply loop back to menu. No vulnerability.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"Initialization of 5 cookies via strdup with hardcoded strings\",\"reason\":\"All strings are hardcoded in .rodata. strdup allocates and copies correctly. msg_size values are hardcoded constants (0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40). No user input involved. No vulnerability.\"},{\"function\":\"menu\",\"description\":\"Prints menu options\",\"reason\":\"Only calls puts() with hardcoded strings. No user input processing. No vulnerability.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the OOB access via BUG-001 can reach useful data structures (FILE* pointers, urandom, cookie_num) at negative offsets from msg[]\",\"reason\":\"The exact memory layout and pointer values at negative offsets depend on runtime state (FILE* pointers are set by libc at runtime). Static analysis confirms the layout but not the pointer values.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic verification: run the binary, input a large unsigned value (e.g., 0x8000000000000000) as cookie index in edit_cookie/read_cookie, and observe what memory is accessed.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether calloc(0,1) in glibc 2.39 returns a usable pointer and how much data read() will actually write\",\"reason\":\"calloc(0,1) behavior is implementation-defined. read() with a huge count will write until the pipe/stdin buffer is exhausted, but the exact amount depends on runtime conditions.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic verification: call create_cookie with size 0, then edit_cookie on that index, and observe heap overflow behavior.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the UAF in BUG-003 can be combined with BUG-001 to access the dangling pointer\",\"reason\":\"The dangling pointer is at index == cookie_num (out of range). BUG-001 allows negative indices (bit 63 set), not indices >= cookie_num. The combination requires careful analysis of whether a negative index can reach the slot at index cookie_num.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic verification: trigger the self-swap case in eat_cookie, then attempt to access the dangling slot via various index values.\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":4,\"verified\":3,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":1,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch in index check: scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") reads unsigned long long, but comparison uses signed jl. Index with bit 63 set (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8 signed) passes the check, then wraps in unsigned multiply to access msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with OOB index k=-8 reads stdout FILE* pointer from .bss, dereferences it via printf(\\\"%s\\\"), and leaks FILE struct content (glibc _IO_magic = 0xFBAD2887).\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf %llu + signed jl comparison\",\"OOB index k=-8 (idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) passes bounds check\",\"msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* (libc pointer)\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", stdout_ptr) dereferences and prints FILE struct content\",\"INFO_LEAK: 4 bytes of _IO_magic leaked\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"OOB array index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\")\",\"how\":\"User inputs 18446744073709551608 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) as cookie index in read_cookie (menu option 4). This value has bit 63 set, passing signed jl check, and wraps in unsigned multiply to access msg[-8].\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"User can reach read_cookie function via menu option 4\",\"necessity\":\"Required to trigger the OOB read path\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"User can input a value with bit 63 set via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") that passes the signed jl bounds check\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the index validation and achieve OOB access\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The OOB index k=-8 resolves to stdout FILE* at .bss offset 0x4020, which is a valid libc pointer\",\"necessity\":\"Required for printf(\\\"%s\\\") to dereference a valid pointer and leak memory\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(msg_base + (-8)*8) = *(0x4060 + 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC0) = *(0x4020)\",\"alias\":\"stdout FILE* global variable at .bss 0x4020\",\"implication\":\"OOB read at k=-8 dereferences the stdout FILE* pointer, leaking the FILE structure's _IO_magic constant (0xFBAD2887) via printf(\\\"%s\\\")\"},{\"expression\":\"msg[-16] = *(msg_base + (-16)*8) = *(0x4060 + 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFF80) = *(0x3FE0)\",\"alias\":\"GOT entry at 0x3FE0 containing a resolved libc address\",\"implication\":\"OOB read at k=-16 dereferences a GOT entry (libc pointer), leaking libc code content via printf(\\\"%s\\\") — defeats ASLR\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie (menu option 4) with OOB index 18446744073709551608 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8, k=-8). Observe leaked bytes in program output.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Leaked bytes 87 28 ad fb (0xFBAD2887 = glibc _IO_magic) in program output\",\"Non-attacker-provided memory content (FILE struct magic) confirms INFO_LEAK\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base address, but the OOB access is relative to .bss (msg array), so PIE does not prevent the OOB read. The leaked FILE struct magic is a constant, not an address.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only at runtime, but the GOT entries still contain resolved libc addresses that can be read via OOB. RELRO does not prevent reading GOT entries.\"},{\"name\":\"ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"ASLR randomizes libc base, but the OOB read dereferences pointers stored in .bss (FILE* pointers, GOT entries) which already contain the randomized addresses. The leak of libc code content via GOT dereference can help defeat ASLR.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x179f-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Index loaded into rdx, cookie_num loaded into rax, compared with 'cmp rdx,rax; jl 17bb'. Signed jl allows negative values (bit 63 set) to pass the check.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17ce\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] accessed via 'lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+msg]; mov rax,[rdx+rax]'. With idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8, rax*8 wraps to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC0, accessing *(0x4060+0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC0) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC execution: read_cookie with idx=18446744073709551608 (k=-8) leaked bytes 87 28 ad fb = 0xFBAD2887 (glibc _IO_2_1_stdout_ magic). This is non-attacker-provided memory content from the stdout FILE structure, confirming INFO_LEAK.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0xFBAD2887 (4 bytes: 87 28 ad fb)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PoC PRIM-001: read_cookie with OOB index k=-8 (idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) leaked stdout FILE struct _IO_magic = 0xFBAD2887. Output: 'Leaked bytes (hex): 8728adfb' and 'FILE _IO_magic: 0xfbad2887'. === PRIM-001: VERIFIED ===\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK verified: OOB read via signedness bypass dereferences stdout FILE* and leaks 4 bytes of FILE struct content (glibc _IO_magic constant 0xFBAD2887). The leak is limited to 4 bytes due to NUL bytes in the magic's high 4 bytes (0x00000000), causing printf(\\\"%s\\\") to stop.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch as PRIM-001. OOB index k=-16 (idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0) accesses msg[-16] = *(0x3FE0), which is a GOT entry containing a resolved libc address.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with OOB index k=-16 reads a GOT entry (libc pointer), dereferences it via printf(\\\"%s\\\"), and leaks libc code content (function prologue bytes). This defeats ASLR by leaking libc code.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf %llu + signed jl comparison\",\"OOB index k=-16 (idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0) passes bounds check\",\"msg[-16] = *(0x3FE0) = GOT entry (resolved libc address)\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", libc_addr) dereferences and prints libc code content\",\"INFO_LEAK: 50 bytes of libc function prologue leaked\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"OOB array index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\")\",\"how\":\"User inputs 18446744073709551600 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0) as cookie index in read_cookie (menu option 4). This value has bit 63 set, passing signed jl check, and wraps in unsigned multiply to access msg[-16].\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"User can reach read_cookie function via menu option 4\",\"necessity\":\"Required to trigger the OOB read path\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"User can input a value with bit 63 set via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") that passes the signed jl bounds check\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the index validation and achieve OOB access\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The OOB index k=-16 resolves to a GOT entry at 0x3FE0, which contains a resolved libc address after dynamic linking\",\"necessity\":\"Required for printf(\\\"%s\\\") to dereference a valid libc pointer and leak code content\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-16] = *(msg_base + (-16)*8) = *(0x4060 + 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFF80) = *(0x3FE0)\",\"alias\":\"GOT entry at 0x3FE0 containing resolved libc address (e.g., 0x00007ffff7dcd200)\",\"implication\":\"OOB read at k=-16 dereferences a GOT entry, leaking libc code content (function prologue: endbr64; push rbp; mov rbp,rsp; ...) via printf(\\\"%s\\\"). This leaks 50 bytes of libc code, defeating ASLR.\"},{\"expression\":\"GOT region 0x3F58-0x4000 contains resolved function pointers\",\"alias\":\"Multiple GOT entries accessible via OOB indices k=-14 to k=-17\",\"implication\":\"Different OOB indices can dereference different GOT entries, potentially leaking various libc code regions\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie (menu option 4) with OOB index 18446744073709551600 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0, k=-16). Observe leaked libc code bytes in program output.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, Full RELRO\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"Leaked bytes containing libc function prologue (f3 0f 1e fa = endbr64, 55 = push rbp, 48 89 e5 = mov rbp,rsp)\",\"50+ bytes of non-attacker-provided libc code content confirms INFO_LEAK\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base, but GOT entries contain resolved libc addresses. The OOB read dereferences these pointers, leaking libc code regardless of PIE.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO resolves all GOT entries at load time and makes GOT read-only. However, the GOT entries still contain valid libc addresses that can be read via OOB. RELRO does not prevent reading GOT entries.\"},{\"name\":\"ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"ASLR randomizes libc base, but the leaked libc code content (function prologue bytes) can be used to identify the libc function and compute the libc base address, defeating ASLR.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17ce\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] accessed with idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0. rax*8 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFF80, accessing *(0x4060+0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFF80) = *(0x3FE0) = GOT entry.\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC execution: read_cookie with idx=18446744073709551600 (k=-16) leaked 50 bytes of libc code: f30f1efa554889e541574989cf4156415541544189f4534889d34883ec2848897dc84d85c9740c4c89cf31d231f6e8bdcf01. This is a libc function prologue (endbr64; push rbp; mov rbp,rsp; push r15; mov r15,rdi; push r14; push r13; push r12; mov r12d,esp; push rbx; mov rbx,rdx; sub rsp,0x28; mov [rbp-0x38],rdi; test r9,r9; je; mov rdi,r9; xor edx,edx; xor esi,esi; call ...). Non-attacker-provided libc code content confirms INFO_LEAK.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"50 bytes of libc code (f30f1efa554889e541574989cf4156415541544189f4534889d34883ec2848897dc84d85c9740c4c89cf31d231f6e8bdcf01)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"PoC PRIM-002: read_cookie with OOB index k=-16 (idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0) leaked 50 bytes of libc code content from a GOT entry dereference. Output: 'Leaked bytes (hex): f30f1efa554889e541574989cf4156415541544189f4534889d34883ec2848897dc84d85c9740c4c89cf31d231f6e8bdcf01' and 'Leaked length: 50 bytes'. === PRIM-002: VERIFIED ===\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK verified: OOB read via signedness bypass dereferences a GOT entry (resolved libc address) and leaks 50 bytes of libc code content (function prologue). This is a significant leak that can defeat ASLR by identifying the libc function and computing the libc base. The leak continues until a NUL byte in the libc code.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch. OOB index k=49 (idx=0x8000000000000031) accesses msg[49] = *(0x41E8) = cookie_num = 5. This value (5) is used as a pointer in printf(\\\"%s\\\"), causing SIGSEGV at address 0x5.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with OOB index k=49 reads cookie_num (value 5) from .bss, treats it as a pointer, and dereferences it via printf(\\\"%s\\\"), causing SIGSEGV.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf %llu + signed jl comparison\",\"OOB index k=49 (idx=0x8000000000000031) passes bounds check\",\"msg[49] = *(0x41