] low 4 bytes (heap pointer low half, ~1.4G) as the write size.\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with OOB index k=-8 targets the stdout FILE structure (writable libc memory) for writing via read(0, stdout_ptr, size). However, the write size (~1.4G) causes read() to return -1 (EFAULT: addr+size exceeds user address space), so no actual write occurs.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: scanf %llu + signed jl comparison\",\"OOB index k=-8 (idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) passes bounds check in edit_cookie\",\"msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* (valid writable libc pointer) -> write target\",\"msg_size[-8] = *(0x4140) = msg[28] low 4 bytes (heap ptr, ~1.4G) -> write size\",\"read(0, stdout_ptr, ~1.4G) -> EFAULT (addr+size exceeds user space)\",\"RELATIVE_WRITE: write target confirmed but write blocked by EFAULT\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"OOB array index (controls write target selection) and write data (via stdin)\",\"how\":\"User inputs 18446744073709551608 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8) as cookie index in edit_cookie (menu option 3). msg[-8] resolves to stdout FILE* (write target). Write data comes from stdin via read(). However, msg_size[-8] is too large, causing EFAULT.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"User can reach edit_cookie function via menu option 3\",\"necessity\":\"Required to trigger the OOB write path\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"User can input a value with bit 63 set via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") that passes the signed jl bounds check\",\"necessity\":\"Required to bypass the index validation and achieve OOB access\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The OOB index k=-8 resolves to stdout FILE* at .bss 0x4020, which is a valid writable libc pointer\",\"necessity\":\"Required for the write target to be a valid writable address\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-04\",\"description\":\"msg_size[-8] must be a small positive integer so that read() size parameter is reasonable and does not cause EFAULT\",\"necessity\":\"Required for read() to actually execute and write data to the target. Currently msg_size[-8] = msg[28] low 4 bytes (heap pointer, ~1.4G), causing EFAULT.\",\"verified\":false},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"No OOB index k exists that satisfies BOTH: msg[k] = valid writable pointer AND msg_size[k] = small positive integer\",\"necessity\":\"This is the blocking condition. All candidate indices with valid pointers have size=0 (->-1, read fails) or size=large heap ptr low half (-> EFAULT). All candidate indices with small sizes have invalid pointer values.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-8] = *(msg_size_base + (-8)*4) = *(0x4160 + 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE0) = *(0x4140)\",\"alias\":\"Low 4 bytes of msg[28] (heap pointer at 0x4060+28*8=0x4150, but 4-byte stride aliases 0x4140 = msg[28] low half)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-8] aliases the low 4 bytes of msg[28]. If msg[28] is a heap pointer (0x0000555555559XXX), the low 4 bytes are 0x55559XXX (~1.4G), causing read() EFAULT.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-4] = *(0x4150) = msg[30] low 4 bytes\",\"alias\":\"Low 4 bytes of msg[30] heap pointer\",\"implication\":\"Same issue: heap pointer low 4 bytes are large, causing EFAULT\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-3] = *(0x4154) = msg[30] high 4 bytes = 0x00005555\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[30] heap pointer (small positive: 0x5555 = 21845)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[-3] = 0x5555 (small positive!) but msg[-3] = *(0x4048) = 0 (NULL, invalid pointer). The size is valid but the pointer is dead.\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[34] = *(0x4160 + 34*4) = *(0x41E8) = cookie_num low 4 bytes = 5\",\"alias\":\"cookie_num low 4 bytes\",\"implication\":\"msg_size[34] = 5 (small positive!) but msg[34] = *(0x4060 + 34*8) = *(0x4170) = msg_size[4] = 0x40 (invalid pointer). Size valid, pointer dead.\"},{\"expression\":\"Two-step write: requires msg[k1] pointing to a writable slot P with valid msg_size[k1], then msg[k2]=P with valid msg_size[k2]\",\"alias\":\"No valid k1 exists (same pointer+size constraint)\",\"implication\":\"Two-step write is impossible because step 1 requires the same valid pointer+size combination that does not exist for any OOB index.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run edit_cookie (menu option 3) with OOB index 18446744073709551608 (k=-8) after creating 24 cookies to fill msg[5..28]. Observe read() return value and write target via GDB.\",\"environment\":\"Linux x86_64, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"GDB: rsi = stdout FILE* (0x7ffff7fa75c0, valid writable libc pointer) — write target confirmed\",\"GDB: rdx = 0x555598af (~1.4G, msg_size[-8]-1) — write size too large\",\"GDB: rax = -1 after read() — EFAULT, no write occurs\",\"Conclusion: write target verified but write execution blocked\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes binary base, but the OOB access is relative to .bss. The write target (stdout FILE*) is a libc pointer stored in .bss, so PIE does not prevent targeting it.\"},{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only, but the write target is the stdout FILE structure in libc data segment, not the GOT. RELRO does not protect the FILE structure.\"},{\"name\":\"ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"ASLR randomizes libc base, but the stdout FILE* pointer in .bss already contains the randomized address. The write target is correctly resolved regardless of ASLR.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size[idx] accessed with idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8. rax*4 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE0, accessing *(0x4160+0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE0) = *(0x4140) = msg[28] low 4 bytes.\"},{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] accessed with idx=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8. rax*8 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC0, accessing *(0x4060+0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC0) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE*.\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de\",\"description\":\"GDB verification: edit_cookie with k=-8 after creating 24 cookies (msg[5..28] filled). At read() call: rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (stdout FILE*, valid writable libc pointer), rdx=0x555598af (msg_size[-8]-1, ~1.4G), rdi=0 (stdin). read() returned -1 (EFAULT: addr 0x7ffff7fa75c0 + size 0x555598af exceeds 47-bit user space). Write target confirmed but no write occurred.\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16e3-0x172b\",\"description\":\"Post-read() NUL write path: when read() returns -1, code checks *(msg[k]+(-1)) = *(stdout_ptr-1) = 0x00 (not 0x0a='\\\\n'), so NUL write is skipped. No write occurs through this path either.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"N/A\",\"description\":\"Complete candidate index enumeration: All OOB indices k with valid msg[k] pointers (k=-8 stdout, k=-6 stdin, k=-4 stderr, k=-16 GOT, k=48 urandom) have msg_size[k]=0 (->size=-1, read fails) or msg_size[k]=heap_ptr_low_half (->size~1.4G, EFAULT). All OOB indices k with small msg_size[k] (k=-3 high_half=0x5555, k=34 cookie_num=5) have msg[k]=NULL or invalid small integer. No index satisfies both constraints. Two-step write impossible (step 1 requires same constraint).\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"GDB verification confirmed: edit_cookie with k=-8 targets stdout FILE* (rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0, valid writable libc pointer) but read() returns -1 (EFAULT) due to size 0x555598af (~1.4G, addr+size exceeds user space). Write target verified, write execution blocked. Complete enumeration of all OOB indices confirms no index satisfies both valid pointer AND small size. Two-step write impossible. === PRIM-004: CANDIDATE (write target confirmed, write blocked) ===\"},\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"notes\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE candidate: The OOB write target (stdout FILE structure) is confirmed as a valid writable libc address via GDB. However, the write size (derived from msg_size[-8] = msg[28] low 4 bytes ≈ 1.4G) causes read() to return EFAULT, preventing actual write execution. Complete enumeration of all OOB indices (negative k: -1 to -16; positive k: 32 to 49) confirms that no index satisfies BOTH constraints (valid writable pointer + small positive size). The two-step write upgrade is also impossible because step 1 requires the same valid pointer+size combination. This primitive remains CANDIDATE (write target verified) but cannot be upgraded to VERIFIED (no actual write occurs). The blocking condition is fundamental: msg[] uses 8-byte stride while msg_size[] uses 4-byte stride, so they never alias the same controlled data in a way that produces both a valid pointer and a small size.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"ARB_WRITE not achievable via BUG-001 alone. The OOB write target is fixed (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* or urandom FILE*), not attacker-controlled. The write target is determined by which .bss global the OOB index aliases, and the attacker cannot change these pointer values. Two-step write to upgrade from RELATIVE_WRITE to ARB_WRITE is impossible because no OOB index provides a valid write execution (all blocked by EFAULT or size=-1). Therefore ARB_WRITE is not a viable primitive for this BUG.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-002\",\"candidate_type\":\"ARB_READ\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"ARB_READ not achievable via BUG-001 alone. The OOB read target (which pointer to dereference) is determined by which .bss global the OOB index aliases. The attacker can choose which global to read (stdout, stdin, stderr, GOT entries, urandom, cookie_num) but cannot specify an arbitrary address Y to read. The read target is always one of the fixed .bss globals. Therefore this is RELATIVE_READ/INFO_LEAK, not ARB_READ.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the INFO_LEAK from PRIM-002 (GOT entry dereference leaking libc code) can be extended to leak full libc addresses (not just code content)\",\"reason\":\"printf(\\\"%s\\\") stops at NUL bytes. The leaked libc code contains NUL bytes (e.g., in call instruction offsets), limiting the leak to ~50 bytes. To leak a full libc pointer, a different approach would be needed (e.g., leaking a pointer value from .bss directly, not dereferencing it).\",\"required_next_step\":\"S4 could explore using the OOB read to leak pointer values directly (e.g., reading msg[-8] = stdout FILE* value as raw bytes, not dereferencing it). However, printf(\\\"%s\\\") always dereferences the pointer, so this would require a different output path.\"}]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
